   "title": "Palo Alto Battlefield National Historical Park (U.S. National Park Service)",
   "text": "An official website of the United States government\nSearch or ask anything\nAsk DOI Anything - How Can We Help?\nHelp me plan a visit to Palo Alto Battlefield National Historical Park\nWhat's on the calendar at Palo Alto Battlefield National Historical Park this week?\nWhat is there to do at Palo Alto Battlefield National Historical Park?\nAre there tours at Palo Alto Battlefield National Historical Park?\nSkip to global NPS navigation\nSkip to this park navigation\nSkip to the main content\nSkip to this park information section\nSkip to the footer section\nNational Park Service\nOpen\nMenu\nPalo Alto Battlefield\nNational Historical Park • Texas\nOn May 8, 1846, U.S. and Mexican troops clashed on the prairie of Palo Alto. The battle was the first in a two-year long war that changed the map of North America. Although the two countries have developed strong bonds and friendly ties since the war ended in 1848, these neighbors continue to contend with the legacy of the war.\nPlan your visit\nAbout the park\nPlan Your Visit\nLearn About the Park\nGet Involved\nInfo\nAlerts 1\nMaps\nCalendar\nFees\n7200 Paredes Line Road, Brownsville, TX 78526\nDirections\nOpen 8:00 AM–5:00 PM today\nHours and seasons\nNo timed entry required\nNo entrance fee\nFees and passes\nHistory & Culture\nDiscover the people, places, and stories that make Palo Alto Battlefield a special place.\nStories\nThe annexation of Texas opened the door to war with Mexico and would test the resolve of the first \"dark horse\" President of the U.S.\nThings to Do\nWhether you've got one hour or a full day to spend at the park, Palo Alto Battlefield has something for everyone.\nLast updated: September 18, 2026\nWas this page helpful?\nYes\nNo\nAn official form of the United States government. Provided by Touchpoints\nBE PREPARED\nBasic Information\nNATURE'S BOUNTY\nExplore the Natural Wonders of Palo Alto\nFUN FOR THE KIDS\nKids & Youth\nTRACE YOUR ROOTS\nU.S.-Mexican War Soldier Database\nPark footer\nCONTACT INFO\nMailing Address:\n600 E. Harrison Street\nRoom 1006\nBrownsville, TX 78520\nPhone:\n956 541-2785\nContact Us\nTOOLS\nFAQ\nSite Index\nEspañol\nSTAY CONNECTED\nExperience Your America.\nDownload the NPS app to navigate the parks on the go.\nSearch\nAccessibility\nPrivacy Policy\nFOIA\nNotices\nContact Palo Alto Battlefield National Historical Park\nNPS FAQ\nNo Fear Act\nDisclaimer\nVulnerability Disclosure Policy\nUSA.gov\nOffice of Civil Rights\nLooking for U.S. government information and services? Visit USA.gov\nConnect with the parks you love by joining the National Park Foundation\nSign Up No Thanks",
